## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Drafts

#### Resumable Uploads
* **Presentation**: [Resumable Uploads](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-httpbis-resumable-uploads-00) (Marios Kleanthous)
* Document: draft-ietf-httpbis-resumable-upload
* Recent revisions resolved several edge cases identified during reviews (including feedback from Julian Reschke and Lucas Pardue). 
* Integration with digest fields was deferred to potential future drafts.
* One final PR remains to be merged before publishing a new version. The editors and chairs agreed the draft is ready for a second 2-week Working Group Last Call (WGLC).

#### Layered Cookies
* Document: draft-ietf-httpbis-layered-cookies
* Authors were not present to give a live update. RFC 6265bis remains in the RFC Editor queue. The chairs will request an update via the mailing list.

#### Purpose Declined HTTP Status Code
* Document: draft-ietf-httpbis-pre-denied
* Presented by Mark Nottingham.
* Status code `419` was selected and incorporated into the latest draft version following outreach regarding potential collisions with existing CMS frameworks.
* The draft is ready for Working Group Last Call.

#### Secondary Certificate Authentication of HTTP Servers
* **Presentation**: [Secondary Certificate Authentication of HTTP servers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-httpbis-secondary-certificate-authentication-of-http-servers-00) (Nick Harper)
* Document: draft-ietf-httpbis-secondary-server-certs
* **Client-Only Settings Negotiation**: Proposed changing setting negotiation so that only the client needs to send the setting to enable unsolicited server certificate frames.
* **Certificate Errors**: Discussed handling of validation failures. Failing validation should lead to discarding the certificate rather than mandatory connection termination. Consensus favoured retaining the `SERVER_CERTIFICATE_INVALID` error code as an informative signal for server operators without forcing connection teardown.
* **Trust Store Rules**: Discussed text requiring clients to use the same trust store settings for secondary certs as the primary TLS handshake. Alessandro Ghedini and Martin Thomson noted clients legitimately use different trust stores per domain. The working group agreed to close the PR without adding the restriction.
* **Scope**: Reaffirmed that secondary certificates apply strictly to the specific HTTP connection on which they are delivered and do not persist across TLS session resumption.
* **Naming**: Agreed to retain `SERVER_CERTIFICATE` rather than renaming back to `CERTIFICATE` to maintain explicit scoping.

